ail because it's something we're  all more familiar with there are  multiple protocols that involve training  more quickly such as plyometric training  ballistic training even compensatory  acceleration but all these things allow  us to develop a greater neural drive for  exploding through movements this can  translate to faster running better  punching sprinting jumping etc and not  necessarily by adding more muscle but by  recruiting more of the fast twitch motor  units that are within that muscle and of  course by building fast twitch muscle  fiber you will potentially add more size  because that's the thicker and stronger  muscle  so how should you implement this what  can you do with all this information  well the first thing is simply to be  more mindful of your tempo when you're  training for a lot of people that's  going to mean really slowing down the  eccentric portion and exploding through  the concentric that's something that a  lot of people use another option of  course is just to go more slowly through  both portions of the movement eccentric  and concentric and we've just discussed  all the many benefits of doing this the  most extreme expression of this is the  quasi-isometric where you can take up to  a minute or even more to perform a  single repetition  just try it out go more slowly i think  you'll be surprised at how this affects  you and how you feel it the next day  there are many other protocols you can  use but there's one in particular that  i've been using for a long time and as  far as i'm aware i made it up i actually  started doing this intuitively when i  first started working out i think it  helped me to gain a lot of strength and  size really quickly and it's not  something i've already heard other  people mention i used to call it time  division i think that's actually a bit  of a lame name so i'm re-dubbing it time  manipulation training which i think  sounds a lot cooler the idea here is  simply to alter and vary your tempo but  